From 9a7500ebaac245b1c67eb3b05c6f3faf8fc9bc52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: c4ffein-work <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 11:39:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Add copysign, float_power, cov, lgamma, gammainc (keras
 master ops)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The ops keras master added since the 3.15.x pin that the backend lacked
(`column_stack` and `matrix_power` were already here), with the numpy
backend's semantics and dtype rules:

- copysign: both signs are sign BITS (x2 = -0.0 gives a negative result,
  the magnitude of x1 = -0.0 is +0.0). tinygrad's abs keeps -0.0, so the
  magnitude is masked and the sign read through bitcasts, all four
  float widths.
- float_power: `power` with both operands promoted to float first, so
  integer inputs with negative exponents have a defined result.
- cov: rows are variables (np.cov's layout); the Cov op's dtype rule
  (int64 -> float64, else result_type(dtype, float)); 1-D / single-
  variable / scalar inputs reduce to a scalar, one observation is nan.
- lgamma: the Lanczos form keras' backend-agnostic `_lgamma` uses (same
  constants, so both agree to float32 rounding), with the unselected
  reflection branch fed a benign argument: at a positive integer
  log(sin(0)) is -inf and its infinite derivative poisoned the `where`
  gradient (0 * inf) — lgamma'(n) is digamma(n) now. Plain log(1 + u)
  instead of the backend's compensated log1p, whose gradient is 0/0 at
  u = 0 (x = 1).
- gammainc: Numerical Recipes' series (x < a + 1) + Lentz continued
  fraction, fixed term counts (200 / 60, float32-exact to a ~ 1000,
  measured), each branch clamped into its convergence region so the
  discarded branch is finite (its nan would poison the gradient). The
  unrolled recurrences are cut every 20 iterations by STACKING the
  carried values into one `contiguous().contiguous_backward()` buffer:
  uncut they render as one C expression past clang's 256-bracket limit
  (forward, and again in the backward pass); cut one by one the
  scheduler materializes every shared intermediate and each iteration
  compiles as its own kernel (75 clang compiles per call shape); the
  stacked cut gives identical segment graphs the kernel cache compiles
  once (5 kernels forward). The iteration index is a carried tensor for
  the same reason. `realize()` would be cheaper but the gradient does
  not flow through realized tensors in tinygrad 0.13 — it silently stops
  at the last segment. First gradient use compiles ~25 kernels (~30 s).

tests/test_ops_beyond_pin.py carries keras master's own test cases for
the five ops (the pinned 3.15.1 suite cannot referee them) plus gradient
receipts against finite differences / digamma, and the MissingOpError
contract; one subprocess, per-check results.

